    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • The Volkswagen Group's Affordable BEV Quartet Has Almost Reached 150,000 Orders\nHow Much Is a 2017 Chevy Camaro ZL1 Worth in 2026?\n21 photos\nPhoto: Instagram | automotive_ambitions\nThe Chevrolet Camaro is bound to return to the automotive kingdom, probably for the 2029 model year, and until then, its predecessors will continue to make headlines.\nIn fact, they will still do that long after the future Camaro is out and about, because some models are on the extremely exciting side of things. You know, just like the one pictured above, which wants you to swipe right on it, after transferring the funds to the vendor and writing your name on the dotted line. Will you do that?\nWell, it all depends on a number of things, like the asking price, which is the biggest one, as well as the overall condition, specification, mileage, model year, and so on. And according to the seller, this is a 2017 model year Chevrolet Camaro in the hot ZL1 configuration , which fought those pesky two-door Hellcats from Dodge back then.\nThis particular ride features a Summit White exterior paint finish, has a black cockpit, benefits from leather upholstery with quite a few suede inserts, has a premium sound system from Bose with nine speakers, a Performance Data and Video Recorder for more fun times at the local racetrack, and a few other bits and bobs that you can see in the photo gallery.\nPhoto: Instagram | automotive_ambitions On top of these, the Chevrolet Camaro ZL1 in question also sports a few modifications. The listing speaks about the new headers, X-pile, mufflers, a DSX tuning flex-fuel sensor, a Rotofab Big Gulp intake, and a 2.3-inch upper pulley. The muscle car in question has been tuned by RPM (Rains Precision Motorsports), and it is said to run from 91-octane to E40 fuel.\nThus, it definitely boasts more power, especially with the latter juice running through its veins, and if you are curious about the magic numbers, then you are not alone, as so are we. However, the vendor hasn’t said anything the output and thrust, so your guess is as good as ours when it comes to this aspect, as well as the revised 0-60 mph acceleration time, the standing quarter-mile performance, and the new top speed.\nBut even with no outside intervention, the Chevrolet Camaro ZL1 was very fast, and still is, even by today’s standards. The 0 to 60 mph (0 to 97 kph) acceleration took a little over three and a half seconds. The standing 1/4-mile was dealt with in less than 12 seconds. In fact, the official number is 11.4 seconds, with an exit speed of 127 mph (204 kph). And that’s definitely something to write home about, isn’t it? Flat-out, this model could do a dizzying 198 mph or 318 kph.\nAs for the firepower, it was supplied by a V8 engine. We are talking about a supercharged mill with a 6.2-liter displacement, which pumped out 650 horsepower with zero mods. That translates to 659 metric horsepower and 485 kilowatts. The thrust was officially rated at 650 pound-feet (881 Nm) of torque. That may be no match to the original Dodge Challenger SRT Hellcat, which had more oomph available via the loud pedal, but the performance was similar, in case you forgot.\nPhoto: Instagram | automotive_ambitions Now, what else must one know about this 2017 model year Chevrolet Camaro ZL1 in order to make up their mind of whether it is worth their hard-earned money or not? Well, the fact that it has a clean title, the vendor says, and at the time of cataloging, the odometer read in the region of 23,000 miles (~37,000 km). An image of the odo, shared in the photo gallery above, reveals that it read 23,437 miles (37,718 km), so it is a low-mileage example given that we are talking about a 2017 model year muscle car.\nAnd if you are wondering how much one of these retails for these days, then you should prepare between $40,000 and $70,000 on average in today’s used car market in the United States of America. You could get one for a bit less than the former number, though it won’t exactly be a clean example, nor a low-mileage one. Some units can fetch close to the six-digit realm, but those are rare. Cars with under 10,000 to 30,000 miles (16,000-48,000 km) with clean accident histories usually go for $60,000 to over $75,000.\nSo, now you know how much a 2017 model year Chevrolet Camaro ZL1 is worth in today’s used car market on our shores. You also know that the pictured example has a clean title. The mileage, some of the equipment, and the engine have also been detailed, though keep in mind that this one features a few modifications. So, how much do you think you will have to cough out if you want to write your name on the dotted line and park it in your driveway?\nThat would be $59,991, which was the asking price at the time of writing. This muscle car is advertised on social media by @automotive_ambitions. Is it worth that kind of money in your opinion? View this post on Instagram A post shared by AUTOMOTIVE AMBITIONS (@automotive_ambitions)\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nchevrolet camaro zl1 Chevrolet Camaro ZL1 for sale used cars USA muscle car v8\nAbout the author: Cristian Gnaticov\nAfter a series of unfortunate events put an end to Cristian's dream of entering a custom built & tuned old-school Dacia into a rally competition, he moved on to drive press cars and write for a living. He's worked for several automotive online journals and now he's back at autoevolution after his first tour in the mid-2000s. Full profile\nUP NEXT Porsche’s Mission S Gets New Wheels and Tires for More Virtual Street Cred",
